## Why This Is Asked
This question targets practical API testing of asynchronous flows, idempotency, and webhook reliability—common in real systems.

## Key Concepts
- Idempotency keys for POST safety
- Async polling and state progression
- Webhook delivery and retry/backoff
- Negative paths and concurrency

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-test sketch for idempotency
async function testIdempotentPost() {
  const key = 'idemp-123';
  const a = await post('/orders', {items:[1,2]}, {headers:{'Idempotency-Key': key}});
  const b = await post('/orders', {items:[1,2]}, {headers:{'Idempotency-Key': key}});
  assert(a.status===202 && b.status===202);
  assert(a.body.order_id===b.body.order_id);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate webhook retries and idempotent processing across failures?
- What tooling would you use to mock downstream services and observe end-to-end retry behavior?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes end-to-end API testing in an event-driven pipeline, focusing on idempotency, schema versioning across tenants, and concurrency safety — realistic in multi-tenant ingestion workloads.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotent ingestion and deduplication across HTTP and Kafka
- Tenant-scoped schema versions and overrides
- End-to-end visibility with distributed tracing
- Race conditions and replay handling in high concurrency
- Tooling: Testcontainers, Pact, k6, Postman

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test: idempotent ingestion using idempotency-key header
async function testIdempotentIngest(client) {
  const payload = {tenant: 't1', version: 2, data: {a:1}};
  const key = 'abc-123';
  await client.post('/ingest', payload, {headers: {'Idempotency-Key': key}});
  await client.post('/ingest', payload, {headers: {'Idempotency-Key': key}});
  // verify only single consume
